      "title": "Adjunct | Criminology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\n\nLocation: Dallas\n\nAbout the Role: The Adjunct position in Criminology offers a unique opportunity to contribute to the academic development of students pursuing studies in criminal justice and related fields. The primary goal of this role is to deliver high-quality instruction that fosters critical thinking, analytical skills, and a comprehensive understanding of criminological theories and practices. The successful candidate will design and implement course materials that engage students in contemporary issues surrounding crime, law enforcement, and the justice system. This role also involves mentoring students, assessing their progress, and contributing to the academic community through collaboration with faculty and participation in departmental activities. Ultimately, the adjunct instructor will play a vital role in preparing students for careers in criminology, law enforcement, policy-making, and research by providing them with a solid educational foundation and practical insights. Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree in Criminology, Criminal Justice, Sociology, or a closely related field. Demonstrated expertise in criminological theories, research methods, and contemporary criminal justice issues. Experience teaching at the college or university level, preferably in criminology or related disciplines. Strong communication and interpersonal skills to effectively engage and support diverse student populations. Eligibility to work in the United States. Responsibilities: Develop and deliver course lectures, seminars, and discussions in criminology and related subjects. Prepare and grade assignments, exams, and other assessments to evaluate student learning outcomes. Provide academic advising and mentorship to students to support their educational and career goals. Stay current with developments in criminology research and incorporate relevant findings into course content. Collaborate with full-time faculty and participate in departmental meetings and curriculum development as needed. Skills: The required and preferred skills are essential for delivering effective and engaging instruction in criminology. Strong communication skills enable the instructor to clearly convey complex concepts and foster meaningful classroom discussions. Analytical skills are used daily to interpret criminological data and research, which inform course content and student assignments. Technological proficiency, especially with online learning platforms, enhances the ability to reach and support students in diverse learning environments. Additionally, interpersonal skills facilitate mentorship and collaboration with students and colleagues, creating a supportive academic community that promotes student success and professional growth. About University: Applicants must be legally authorized to work in the United States. Misericordia University is not able to sponsor or transfer employment visas for this position. Misericordia University, founded by the Sisters of Mercy in 1924, is a comprehensive institution offering bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree programs. Located in Northeastern Pennsylvania near the Pocono Mountains, the university is approximately 2.5 hours from both New York City and Philadelphia. Misericordia is recognized for its commitment to academic excellence, professional preparation, and service to others, rooted in the values of the Sisters of Mercy.",
